    "text": "A refrigerator keeps food cold by moving heat from inside to outside, like carrying warmth away in a bucket. A special liquid called refrigerant flows through pipes. First, it absorbs heat from inside the fridge and turns into a gas. Then a compressor squeezes that gas, making it hot. Next, the hot gas flows through coils on the back or bottom, where it releases heat into your kitchen and turns back into a liquid. Finally, it travels back inside to soak up more heat. This cycle repeats over and over. Because heat keeps getting removed, the inside stays cold, and your food stays fresh."
